B.Mk=new A.hS("root",B.f5,"Triage Station","What type of problem are you experiencing?","Select the symptom that best describes what is happening right now:",B.Vl)
B.My=new A.cI("No, the green ON LED is completely dark","The microcontroller is receiving zero electricity.","power_led_dark",null)
B.X5=s(["Unplug the USB cable immediately.","Disconnect DC motors, servos, or relays from the Arduino 5V pin.","Power mechanical actuators using an external battery pack or 9V barrel jack.","Add a 100uF electrolytic capacitor across the power and GND rails to smooth voltage drops."],t.s)
B.mA=new A.Cw(1,"caution")
B.N8=new A.e5("Current Overload / Brownout Reset","Drawing more current than the computer USB port can safely supply (>500mA).","Inductive loads like motors and high-current coils draw a massive inrush spike when turning on. This drops the 5V bus voltage below 4.3V, triggering the Arduino\u2019s brownout reset circuit.",B.X5,"Plug USB back in without motors. Verify the green ON LED stays solidly illuminated.",B.mA)
B.MA=new A.cI("Yes, but it dims or reboots repeatedly","The board keeps restarting when motor or buzzer activates.",null,B.N8)
B.Ul=s(["DISCONNECT USB CABLE RIGHT NOW! Do not leave it plugged in.","Inspect your breadboard: check if a jumper wire connects the red (+) rail directly to the blue (-) rail.","Look for stray bent component leads touching each other.","Let the board cool down for 2 minutes before plugging back in."],t.s)
B.Mg=new A.Cw(2,"criticalWarning")
B.N0=new A.e5("Critical Short Circuit Detected!","A direct bridge exists between 5V and GND, or an output pin is shorted.","Electricity always takes the path of least resistance. When 5V touches GND directly with no resistor or load, infinite current flows, rapidly heating the onboard voltage regulator or ATmega328P chip.",B.Ul,"With breadboard disconnected, plug in Arduino. Touch chip\u2014it should stay completely cool.",B.Mg)
B.MH=new A.cI("Yes, green ON LED is on, but the chip feels hot!","The black square or rectangle chip feels warm or burning to touch.",null,B.N0)
B.V9=s([B.My,B.MA,B.MH],t.me)
B.Mp=new A.hS("power_start",B.f5,"Power Inspection",'Is the small green LED labeled "ON" lit on your Arduino board?',"Look near the center of the green Arduino circuit board.",B.V9)
B.Va=s(["Keep USB plugged in to Arduino only.","On the breadboard, check every component: verify no wire connects (+) and (-) rails together.","Check IC chips: make sure they are not inserted backwards.","Reconnect breadboard wires one by one to find which component causes the shutdown."],t.s)
B.Nd=new A.e5("Breadboard Short Circuit","There is a short circuit somewhere on your breadboard pulling the 5V rail to ground.","The Arduino Uno has a built-in resettable polyfuse that trips when breadboard wiring draws too much current, shutting off power to save your computer.",B.Va,"Plug 5V and GND wires back into breadboard; green ON LED must remain lit.",B.mA)
B.Mv=new A.cI("The green ON LED turns on now!","Arduino lights up when breadboard is unplugged.",null,B.Nd)
B.Wb=s(["Try a different USB port on your computer (prefer ports directly on the motherboard, not hubs).","Swap the USB-B cable for a known good data cable.",'Check if your computer displays a "USB device drawing too much power" notification.'],t.s)
B.bD=new A.Cw(0,"normal")
B.N4=new A.e5("USB Cable or Computer Port Failure",'Using a "charge-only" USB cable, a dead computer port, or a tripped computer USB port.',"Some cheap USB cables only contain the 2 power wires and have broken copper traces, or the computer disabled the USB port due to previous overload.",B.Wb,"Plug into a different USB port with a known data cable. Green ON LED should glow.",B.bD)
B.Mt=new A.cI("Still completely dark even with nothing plugged into Arduino","Board is dark with just the USB cable attached.",null,B.N4)
B.VO=s([B.Mv,B.Mt],t.me)
B.Mq=new A.hS("power_led_dark",B.f5,"USB Power Check","What happens when you unplug the breadboard power wires from the Arduino?","Remove the 5V and GND wires connecting Arduino to the breadboard, leaving only the USB cable.",B.VO)
B.MM=new A.cI("An LED won\u2019t illuminate","The LED stays dark when the code runs digitalWrite(pin, HIGH).","wiring_led",null)
B.X1=s(["Peel off the round yellow protective sticker on top of the buzzer.","Check the pins: the longer lead or the side marked with a (+) must connect to the Arduino signal pin.","The shorter lead must connect to the GND rail."],t.s)
B.N3=new A.e5("Buzzer Polarity or Protective Sticker","Active buzzers are polarized and frequently ship with a yellow factory seal.","An active buzzer contains an internal oscillating circuit. If reverse-biased, current cannot flow through the transistor oscillator. Also, the round yellow sticker dampens sound by 90%.",B.X1,"Touch the (+) lead to 5V and (-) lead to GND directly. It should emit a sharp loud beep.",B.bD)
B.MJ=new A.cI("Active Buzzer makes no sound or only clicks","Buzzer is silent or produces a faint muted click.",null,B.N3)
B.UE=s(["In your Arduino sketch, change pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT); to pinMode(buttonPin, INPUT_PULLUP);.","Wire the button between the digital pin and GND.","Verify in code that pressing the button now reads LOW."],t.s)
B.N1=new A.e5("Floating Digital Input Pin","Digital input pin has no pull-up or pull-down resistor.","A digital input pin with no resistor floats between 0V and 5V. It acts like an antenna picking up static electromagnetic fields in the air.",B.UE,"Wave your hand over the breadboard. The button should no longer trigger randomly.",B.bD)
B.MG=new A.cI("Pushbutton triggers erratically or never triggers","Button seems to have a mind of its own or waving hands triggers it.",null,B.N1)
B.Xm=s(["Look closely at the red and blue colored lines on the edges of your breadboard.","If there is a gap in the center of the line, electricity stops there.","Insert short jumper wires across the middle gap from (+) to (+) and (-) to (-)."],t.s)
B.N2=new A.e5("Split Breadboard Power Rails","Full-size breadboards have a break in the power rails halfway down.","Many full-length breadboards divide their red (+) and blue (-) power buses in the exact center so you can use dual voltages. If not bridged, the bottom half is completely disconnected.",B.Xm,"Move LED circuit to the far end of the breadboard. It should now light up.",B.bD)
B.MF=new A.cI("Components on one half of breadboard work, other half dead","Power is on one side, but anything on the far half receives no power.",null,B.N2)
B.Y2=s([B.MM,B.MJ,B.MG,B.MF],t.me)
B.Mr=new A.hS("wiring_start",B.ef,"Wiring & Component Triage","Which component is refusing to work?",null,B.Y2)
B.XK=s(["Unplug the LED from the breadboard.","Rotate the LED 180 degrees.","Ensure the longer leg (Anode) connects to the Arduino digital pin (through a resistor).","Ensure the shorter leg (Cathode) connects to the GND rail."],t.s)
B.Nb=new A.e5("Reversed LED Polarity (Anode vs Cathode)","Light Emitting Diodes only conduct electricity in ONE direction.","A diode is a one-way electrical valve. Current must enter the Anode (longer leg) and exit the Cathode (shorter leg, flat side on the plastic rim). When reversed, current is blocked.",B.XK,"Run the Blink sketch. The LED should now flash brightly.",B.bD)
B.MQ=new A.cI("I am not sure which leg is which","Both legs look similar or were clipped.",null,B.Nb)
B.XI=s(["Discard this LED\u2014once burned out, it cannot be fixed.","Take a fresh LED from your kit.","Always insert a 220-ohm (Red-Red-Brown) or 330-ohm (Orange-Orange-Brown) resistor in series with the LED."],t.s)
B.MX=new A.e5("Burned Out LED (Missing Current Limiting Resistor)","Excessive current burned the internal LED bonding wire.","Without a resistor (e.g. 220 or 330 ohms), an LED attempts to draw infinite current from the 5V pin. Within milliseconds, the internal silicon junction overheats and pops.",B.XI,"Wire fresh LED with a 220-ohm resistor. Verify it lights up safely.",B.mA)
B.MT=new A.cI("Orientation is correct, but there is no resistor","LED is wired directly between Arduino pin and GND.",null,B.MX)
B.Xg=s(["Trace the wire from the breadboard back to the Arduino Uno header.","Read the exact pin number printed next to the header socket.","Update your sketch to match the physical pin, or move the wire to match your code."],t.s)
B.N9=new A.e5("Software Pin vs Hardware Pin Mismatch","The pin defined in code does not match the hole the jumper wire is plugged into.","If your sketch has int ledPin = 13; but the jumper wire is plugged into digital header hole 12, pin 13 is toggling in empty space.",B.Xg,"Upload the sketch again and verify the LED responds to code commands.",B.bD)
B.MK=new A.cI("Resistor and orientation are good, but still dark","Wiring looks correct but code does nothing.",null,B.N9)
B.Xn=s([B.MQ,B.MT,B.MK],t.me)
B.Mn=new A.hS("wiring_led",B.ef,"LED Inspection","How is the LED oriented in the breadboard?",null,B.Xn)
B.V2=s(["Look at the line of code HIGHLIGHTED in red, then look at the line DIRECTLY ABOVE IT.","Check the end of that line: add a semicolon ;.","Click the Verify checkmark button to recompile."],t.s)
B.N5=new A.e5("Missing Semicolon (;)","In C++, every single statement must terminate with a semicolon.","The compiler reads ahead until it hits a semicolon. When one is missing, it tries to merge two statements together and reports an error on the NEXT line.",B.V2,'Arduino IDE console reports "Done compiling" with sketch size.',B.bD)
B.MV=new A.cI("error: expected ';' before '...'","A missing semicolon at the end of a statement.",null,B.N5)
B.Xr=s(["Check spelling and capitalization of the identifier listed in the error message.","Verify that the variable was declared at the top of the sketch (global scope) or inside the current function.","Check common built-ins: OUTPUT (all caps), HIGH (all caps), INPUT_PULLUP (all caps)."],t.s)
B.N7=new A.e5("Undeclared Variable or Typo (Case Sensitivity)","C++ is strictly case-sensitive. ledPin, LedPin, and LEDPin are 3 totally different names.","If you defined int ledPin = 9; in setup() or at the top, typing digitalWrite(LedPin, HIGH); will fail because the capital L doesn\u2019t match.",B.Xr,"Fix the typo and click Verify. The error will disappear.",B.bD)
B.ML=new A.cI("error: '...' was not declared in this scope","The compiler does not recognize a variable or function name.",null,B.N7)
B.WF=s(["In the Arduino IDE menu, go to: Sketch -> Include Library -> Manage Libraries...",'Type the library name into the search bar (e.g. "LiquidCrystal_I2C" or "DHT sensor library").','Click "Install" (and "Install All" if it asks for dependencies).',"Re-verify your sketch."],t.s)
B.Nc=new A.e5("Missing External Library","The #include header file cannot be found in your Arduino libraries folder.","Sensors like the DHT11, RFID RC522, and LCD backpacks require third-party C++ libraries to communicate with the hardware.",B.WF,"Sketch compiles cleanly without missing header errors.",B.bD)
B.MS=new A.cI("fatal error: ...h: No such file or directory","The sketch references a library that is not installed.",null,B.Nc)
B.WD=s(["In Arduino IDE, press Ctrl+T (or Cmd+T on Mac) to Auto-Format your code.","Auto-formatting indents code blocks. Look for code that is indented all the way to the right.","Click on an opening brace {; the IDE will highlight its matching closing brace }.","Add the missing } where the block should finish."],t.s)
B.MY=new A.e5("Unmatched Curly Braces { }","Every opening curly brace { must have an exact matching closing curly brace }.","Curly braces define code blocks for functions, if-statements, and loops. If one is missing, the compiler thinks loop() never ended.",B.WD,"Auto-format the code again and verify proper indentation and clean compilation.",B.bD)
B.MB=new A.cI("error: expected '}' at end of input","Mismatched curly braces in the sketch.",null,B.MY)
B.VJ=s([B.MV,B.ML,B.MS,B.MB],t.me)
B.Mo=new A.hS("compile_start",B.jn,"Compiler Error Decoder","What does the orange error message in the Arduino IDE console say?",null,B.VJ)
B.MC=new A.cI("avrdude: stk500_recv(): programmer is not responding","The computer cannot communicate with the Arduino bootloader.","upload_stk500",null)
B.WV=s(["Ensure your USB cable is a DATA cable, not a phone charging-only cable.","Download and install the CH340 USB driver (available on Arduino/WCH website).","Restart the Arduino IDE after installing the driver.","Check Tools -> Port: select the new COM port that appears."],t.s)
B.MZ=new A.e5("Missing CH340 Driver or Power-Only Cable","Clone Arduino boards use a CH340 USB-to-Serial chip requiring a driver, or USB cable has no data wires.","Original Arduinos use an ATmega16U2 for USB, but many student kits use the CH340 chip. Windows/Mac requires the CH340 driver to create a virtual COM port.",B.WV,"Tools -> Port will display an active COM/tty port for your board.",B.bD)
B.Mz=new A.cI("Tools -> Port menu is grayed out or shows no COM ports","Computer does not recognize any USB device plugged in.",null,B.MZ)
B.Wp=s(["Close the Serial Monitor window (magnifying glass in top right).","Close any other open Arduino IDE windows or 3D printer slicing tools.","Unplug and replug the USB cable."],t.s)
B.N_=new A.e5("Serial Port Lockout","The Serial Monitor or a second Arduino IDE window is locking the COM port.","Operating systems only allow one program to hold a serial COM port open at a time. Uploading requires exclusive hardware access.",B.Wp,"Click Upload; the TX and RX LEDs on the Uno will flash rapidly during upload.",B.bD)
B.Mu=new A.cI("Upload error: Serial port in use or permission denied","Another application is holding the serial port open.",null,B.N_)
B.XZ=s([B.MC,B.Mz,B.Mu],t.me)
B.Ms=new A.hS("upload_start",B.hz,"Upload & Serial Triage","What error message appears when you click the Upload arrow button?",null,B.XZ)
B.TR=s(["Unplug the wires from Pin 0 and Pin 1 completely.","Click the Upload button in the Arduino IDE.",'Wait for "Done uploading" message.',"Plug the wires back into pins 0 and 1."],t.s)
B.Na=new A.e5("Hardware Serial Interference (Pins 0 & 1 Conflict)","Pins 0 (RX) and 1 (TX) are directly wired to the USB chip that uploads your code.","When you upload code, binary bytes stream across pins 0 and 1. If an external Bluetooth module, sensor, or wire is connected to those pins, it scrambles the upload data stream!",B.TR,"Sketch uploads in 3 seconds once pins 0 and 1 are temporarily freed.",B.bD)
B.MR=new A.cI("Yes, I have Bluetooth, GPS, or sensors wired to pins 0 and 1","Pins 0 and 1 are connected to breadboard components.",null,B.Na)
B.Ui=s(["Go to Tools -> Board -> Arduino AVR Boards.",'Ensure "Arduino Uno" is selected.',"Go to Tools -> Port and re-select your active COM port.",'Press the red reset button on the Arduino right as the IDE status changes from "Compiling" to "Uploading".'],t.s)
B.MW=new A.e5("Wrong Board Model Selected in Tools Menu","Selected board type does not match the connected hardware.",'If you have an Arduino Uno, but Tools -> Board is set to "Arduino Nano" or "Arduino Mega", the avrdude upload protocol uses the wrong baud rate (e.g. 57600 instead of 115200).',B.Ui,'Upload succeeds with "Done uploading" status.',B.bD)
B.MI=new A.cI("No, pins 0 and 1 are empty, but it still fails","Pins 0 and 1 are free.",null,B.MW)
B.VU=s([B.MR,B.MI],t.me)
B.Ml=new A.hS("upload_stk500",B.hz,"Bootloader Communication Check","Are there any wires plugged into Digital Pins 0 (RX) and 1 (TX) on your Arduino?","Look at the first two digital pin sockets on the Arduino board labeled 0 and 1.",B.VU)
B.Mh=new A.rA(4,"logic")
B.VZ=s(["Verify that TRIG pin in code matches the physical wire to TRIG on the sensor.","Verify that ECHO pin in code matches the physical wire to ECHO on the sensor.","Ensure your code has a delay(50); or delay(60); inside loop() after each reading.","Ensure VCC is connected to 5V (HC-SR04 will not work reliably on 3.3V)."],t.s)
B.Ne=new A.e5("Ultrasonic Pin Swap or Ping Rate Too Fast","TRIG and ECHO pins swapped, or sensor triggered without 50ms delay between pings.","The HC-SR04 requires a 10us trigger pulse on TRIG, then listens for sound reflection on ECHO. If swapped, no pulse is emitted. If pinged faster than 20Hz, sound echoes collide in the room.",B.VZ,"Place your hand 10cm from sensor; serial monitor should read ~10 cm accurately.",B.bD)
B.MO=new A.cI("Ultrasonic Distance Sensor reads 0 or 3000 cm constantly","HC-SR04 sonar sensor values are pegged at max or min.",null,B.Ne)
B.X2=s(["Use a small Phillips screwdriver to slowly turn the blue brass trimmer screw on the back of the LCD backpack.","Turn until letters become crisp and dark while the blue background turns clear.","If still blank, run the I2C Scanner sketch to check if your backpack address is 0x27 or 0x3F.","Update LiquidCrystal_I2C lcd(0x27, 16, 2); with the scanned hex address."],t.s)
B.N6=new A.e5("Contrast Potentiometer or I2C Hex Address","Backlight contrast trimmer is turned too far, or I2C address is 0x3F instead of 0x27.","The I2C backpack on the back of the LCD has a small blue potentiometer that controls liquid crystal contrast. If set to maximum, all pixel blocks appear solid black.",B.X2,"Text prints clearly across both 16-character lines.",B.bD)
B.MP=new A.cI("I2C LCD1602 Screen shows dark solid rectangles or nothing","The display has power but no text appears.",null,B.N6)
B.XF=s(["Check outer legs: Leg 1 must go to 5V, Leg 3 must go to GND.","Check center leg: Must connect to A0, A1, A2, A3, A4, or A5.","In code, ensure you are using analogRead(A0), not digitalRead(A0)."],t.s)
B.Nf=new A.e5("Missing Voltage Divider Reference or Digital Pin Connected","Potentiometer missing 5V or GND outer leg, or wired to digital pin instead of analog.","A potentiometer is a variable voltage divider. Outer legs must connect to 5V and GND. The center wiper pin must connect to an ANALOG IN pin (A0-A5), not digital pin 0-13.",B.XF,"Turning knob from left to right should smoothly sweep readings from 0 to 1023.",B.bD)
B.MD=new A.cI("Analog sensor (Potentiometer / LDR) always reads 1023 or 0","Turning knob or shining light has zero effect on readings.",null,B.Nf)
B.W1=s([B.MO,B.MP,B.MD],t.me)
B.Mm=new A.hS("logic_start",B.Mh,"Sensor & Logic Diagnostics","Which sensor or display is behaving erratically?",null,B.W1)
